{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Arthur blinked, the sunlight strangely vibrant. He sat up in bed, a wave of disorientation washing over him. The room was… too perfect. Too symmetrical. The floral wallpaper, the neatly arranged books, even the dust motes dancing in the sunbeams seemed meticulously placed. He reached out to touch his bedside table, and his fingers passed right through it. A cold dread seized him. He looked at his hands – pixelated, blocky, undeniably digital. A notification popped up in his vision: \"Welcome, Player 427. You have entered Aethelgard. Quest: Find the Lost Amulet of Eldoria.\" He scrambled out of bed, his legs feeling strangely stiff, and stumbled towards the door. The world outside was even more surreal: vibrant greens, impossibly blue skies, and villagers frozen in repetitive, looping actions. A quest log floated in his sight, detailing tasks and objectives. This wasn't his life. This was a game.\n\n***\n\nHe spent the day navigating the pre-determined paths of Aethelgard, a growing sense of despair settling in. Every interaction felt scripted, every conversation a pre-programmed response. He battled a goblin – a surprisingly easy fight, almost insultingly so – and received a generic reward: \"Goblin Scrap x5.\" The quest log urged him onward, directing him towards a dark forest rumored to house the amulet. He tried to break the code, to find a glitch, a way to escape this digital prison, but everything seemed flawlessly designed. He was trapped, a pawn in someone else’s elaborate fantasy. As the sun began to set, casting long, artificial shadows, Arthur slumped against a digital oak tree, the weight of his reality – or lack thereof – crushing him. The notification blinked: \"Day Cycle Complete. Rest is advised.\" He felt a flicker of rebellion, a desperate urge to scream, but he knew it was futile. He was just a player, and the game would continue.\n\n\n\